WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.300
An animal cell has sorted its chromosomes into two sets.

2
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.300
Now it must cut itself in two.

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:17.380
But where? The furrow, the inward fold that does the cutting, forms midway between

4
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.820
the two sets. That position is not an accident.

5
00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:28.080
The spindle, the array of microtubules that pulled the chromosomes apart, sends the signal.

6
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:32.113
The signal is a small switch protein called RhoA.

7
00:00:32.773 --> 00:00:37.533
It is on when it holds GTP, and off when it holds GDP.

8
00:00:38.773 --> 00:00:42.813
At the equator, an enzyme called Ect2 switches it on.

9
00:00:43.753 --> 00:00:46.853
Ect2 swaps GDP for GTP.

10
00:00:48.313 --> 00:00:50.853
Researchers then made active RhoA visible.

11
00:00:51.493 --> 00:00:57.653
They fused a green fluorescent protein to a fragment that binds only GTP-bound RhoA.

12
00:00:58.473 --> 00:01:03.153
The glow marks where RhoA is on, not where the protein simply sits.

13
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:08.587
Active RhoA gathers in a bounded band at the equator.

14
00:01:09.327 --> 00:01:12.947
The band appears one to four minutes before the furrow.

15
00:01:13.707 --> 00:01:17.307
This was seen in sea urchin embryos and in frog embryos.

16
00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:21.827
Next, the researchers blocked actin assembly with drugs.

17
00:01:22.627 --> 00:01:25.327
In sea urchin embryos, the band still formed.

18
00:01:25.927 --> 00:01:30.267
So RhoA switches on before the actin ring, and does not need it.

19
00:01:30.967 --> 00:01:36.987
In sea urchin cells already furrowing, the researchers pushed the spindle aside with

20
00:01:36.987 --> 00:01:42.767
a blunt needle. The band relocated over three to five minutes, and the furrow

21
00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:46.907
followed it. Move the spindle, and the signal moves first.

22
00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:51.747
The delay argues against the spindle simply dragging the cortex along.

23
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:57.940
Active RhoA turns on formins, which build long actin filaments.

24
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:04.040
It also promotes myosin II, a motor that pulls actin filaments past each other.

25
00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.920
Together they make a contractile ring under the membrane, and as it tightens, the

26
00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.880
furrow ingresses. Ingression leaves a thin bridge between the daughters.

27
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:22.400
Later, in several animal cell systems, ESCRT-III assembles in the bridge, constricts

28
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.460
it and cuts it.

29
00:02:24.683 --> 00:02:31.083
These timings come from large embryonic cells, so do not assume the same minutes in

30
00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:36.983
a human cell. The drawstring ring is a standard cartoon, and filament arrangement

31
00:02:36.983 --> 00:02:38.383
differs between systems.

32
00:02:39.363 --> 00:02:44.663
Keep the rule: the spindle places the signal, and the furrow follows the signal.
